<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>AI Visibility Tracker Report</title>
<style>
:root{--bg:#fafaf7;--fg:#1c1b19;--muted:#6b6860;--card:#fff;--line:#e3e0d8;--accent:#1f6f5c;--bar:#9cc9bd;--warn-bg:#fff1d6;--warn-fg:#6b4300;--mock-bg:#fde4e1;--mock-fg:#7a1f14}
@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){:root{--bg:#161614;--fg:#ecebe6;--muted:#9a978e;--card:#1f1f1c;--line:#34332e;--accent:#6fcfb6;--bar:#2f5a50;--warn-bg:#3a2f12;--warn-fg:#f1d391;--mock-bg:#44201b;--mock-fg:#ffc9c0}}
*{box-sizing:border-box}
body{margin:0;background:var(--bg);color:var(--fg);font:15px/1.5 system-ui,-apple-system,"Segoe UI",sans-serif}
main{max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;padding:24px 16px 64px}
h1{font-size:1.6rem;margin:0 0 4px}h2{font-size:1.2rem;margin:36px 0 8px}h3{font-size:1rem;margin:20px 0 6px}
p,li{max-width:75ch}.muted{color:var(--muted)}.small{font-size:.85rem}
.banner{padding:12px 14px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;font-weight:500}
.banner.mock{background:var(--mock-bg);color:var(--mock-fg)}.banner.real{background:var(--warn-bg);color:var(--warn-fg)}
.cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(150px,1fr));gap:10px;margin:12px 0}
.card{background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px}
.card b{display:block;font-size:1.35rem}
.scroll{overflow-x:auto}
table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px}
th,td{padding:7px 9px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);text-align:left;vertical-align:top}
th{font-size:.8rem;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted)}td.num,th.num{text-align:right}
.rate{font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;white-space:nowrap}.rate b{font-size:1rem}
.bar{display:block;position:relative;height:6px;background:var(--line);border-radius:3px;margin-top:4px;min-width:90px}
.bar i{position:absolute;top:0;height:6px;background:var(--bar);border-radius:3px}
.bar b{position:absolute;top:-2px;width:3px;height:10px;background:var(--accent);margin-left:-1px}
.sub{font-size:.75rem;color:var(--muted)}
code{background:var(--line);padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;font-size:.85em}
details{margin:8px 0}summary{cursor:pointer;color:var(--accent)}
.reps li{margin:2px 0}
.hm td{text-align:center;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;padding:4px 6px}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
<h1>AI Visibility Tracker: {{ cfg.market.city }} dental practices</h1>
<p class="muted">Batch <code>{{ d.meta.batch }}</code> · generated {{ generated }} · location set to {{ d.meta.location }}</p>

{% if anonymized %}<p class="small muted">Anonymized version: the tracked practices are labelled Practice A to {{ cfg.brands[-1].name[-1] }}, and other businesses are not listed.</p>{% endif %}
{% if d.meta.is_mock %}
<div class="banner mock">SYNTHETIC DATA. This report was produced with <code>--mock</code>: the engine answers are generated by a made-up model and the practices are fictional. It demonstrates the pipeline. None of these numbers measure any real engine or business.</div>
{% else %}
<div class="banner real">Real batch, small sample. {{ d.meta.n_runs }} runs between {{ d.meta.first_run[:10] }} and {{ d.meta.last_run[:10] }}. Wide intervals are expected and are the honest result. Practices were chosen as a convenience sample from public directories; this is not a ranking.</div>
{% endif %}

{% if disabled %}
<p class="small muted">Engines not run: {% for k, why in disabled.items() %}{{ labels.get(k, k) }} ({{ why }}){% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}.</p>
{% endif %}

<div class="cards">
  <div class="card"><span class="muted small">Runs stored</span><b>{{ d.meta.n_runs }}</b></div>
  <div class="card"><span class="muted small">Engines</span><b>{{ d.engines|length }}</b></div>
  <div class="card"><span class="muted small">Prompts × repetitions</span><b>{{ cfg.prompts|length }} × {% for e in d.engines %}{{ d.engine_summary[e].reps }}{% if not loop.last %} / {% endif %}{% endfor %}</b><span class="sub">per engine, in column order</span></div>
  <div class="card"><span class="muted small">API cost</span><b>${{ '%.2f'|format(d.total_cost) }}</b>{% if d.meta.is_mock %}<span class="sub">mock: nothing spent</span>{% endif %}</div>
</div>

<h2>1. How often each practice is named</h2>
<p>Share of answers that name the practice, per engine, with a 95% Wilson interval. The small line under each rate is a second interval that treats the <em>prompt</em> as the unit (prompt-level bootstrap), because repeated answers to the same prompt are not independent. It is wider than the Wilson interval when practices do much better on some prompts than others; with {{ cfg.prompts|length }} prompts it is itself rough. Only answers that were actually returned count; errors and "no AI Overview shown" are excluded from the denominator.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Practice</th>{% for e in d.engines %}<th>{{ labels.get(e, e) }}<br><span class="sub">n = {{ d.engine_summary[e].scored }} answers</span></th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>
<tbody>
{% for b in cfg.brands %}
<tr><td>{{ b.name }}</td>
{% for e in d.engines %}{% set r = d.rates[b.id][e] %}
<td><span class="rate"><b>{{ r.rate|pct }}</b> <span class="sub">{{ r.k }}/{{ r.n }}</span></span>
<span class="sub">95% CI {{ r.lo|pct }}–{{ r.hi|pct }}</span>
<span class="bar"><i style="left:{{ r.lo|bar }}%;width:{{ ((r.hi - r.lo) if r.n else 0)|bar }}%"></i><b style="left:{{ r.rate|bar }}%"></b></span>
<span class="sub">{% if r.cluster_lo == r.cluster_lo %}by prompt: {{ r.cluster_lo|pct }}–{{ r.cluster_hi|pct }}{% else %}by prompt: n/a (same rate on every prompt){% endif %}</span></td>
{% endfor %}</tr>
{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>

<h3>Cited as a source</h3>
<p class="small muted">Share of answers whose cited URLs include the practice's own website. This needs a correct domain in the config; Perplexity counts only sources the answer marks with [n].</p>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Practice</th>{% for e in d.engines %}<th>{{ labels.get(e, e) }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>
<tbody>
{% for b in cfg.brands %}
<tr><td>{{ b.name }}</td>
{% for e in d.engines %}{% set r = d.rates[b.id][e] %}
<td class="rate"><b>{{ r.cited_rate|pct }}</b> <span class="sub">{{ r.k_cited }}/{{ r.n }} · {{ r.cited_lo|pct }}–{{ r.cited_hi|pct }}</span></td>
{% endfor %}</tr>
{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>

<h3>Share of voice among tracked practices</h3>
<p class="small muted">Each practice's share of all mentions of the tracked list. Businesses outside the list are invisible to the regex layer (the optional LLM judge lists them), so this is not market share.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Practice</th>{% for e in d.engines %}<th class="num">{{ labels.get(e, e) }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>
<tbody>
{% for b in cfg.brands %}
<tr><td>{{ b.name }}</td>{% for e in d.engines %}<td class="num">{{ d.sov[e][b.id]|pct }}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>
{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>2. Why one answer is not a result</h2>
<p>Each prompt was sent several times to each engine ({% for e in d.engines %}{{ labels[e] }}: {{ d.engine_summary[e].reps }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}). If answers were stable, the repetitions would agree. This is how much they did{% if d.meta.is_mock %} (synthetic data: this only shows the calculation, not how real engines behave){% endif %}.</p>

<h3>All businesses named</h3>
{% if d.name_overlap %}
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th class="num">Mean overlap between two repetitions</th><th class="num">Business-prompt pairs</th><th class="num">...named in every repetition</th><th class="num">...named in only one repetition</th><th class="num">Distinct business names</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{% for e in d.engines %}{% if d.name_overlap.get(e) %}{% set v = d.name_overlap[e] %}
<tr><td>{{ labels.get(e, e) }}<br><span class="sub">{{ v.answers }} answers judged</span></td>
<td class="num">{{ v.mean_jaccard|pct }}<br><span class="sub">per prompt {{ v.per_prompt_min|pct }}–{{ v.per_prompt_max|pct }}</span></td>
<td class="num">{{ v.name_prompt_pairs }}</td>
<td class="num">{{ v.pairs_in_every_rep }} ({{ ((v.pairs_in_every_rep / v.name_prompt_pairs) if v.name_prompt_pairs else None)|pct }})</td>
<td class="num">{{ v.pairs_in_one_rep }} ({{ ((v.pairs_in_one_rep / v.name_prompt_pairs) if v.name_prompt_pairs else None)|pct }})</td>
<td class="num">{{ v.distinct_names }}</td></tr>
{% endif %}{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small muted">Names come from the LLM judge plus the tracked practices. Overlap is the Jaccard similarity of the sets of business names in two repetitions of the same prompt, averaged over all pairs (100% = identical every time). A "business-prompt pair" is one business named at least once for one prompt and engine; a business named for two prompts counts twice, while "distinct business names" counts it once. Names are compared as written (lowercased, no punctuation), so one business under two spellings overstates the disagreement a little. The judge is a Claude model and one of the engines is too.</p>
{% else %}
<p class="small muted">Not available: it needs LLM-judge verdicts (<code>python -m vistrack judge</code>); without them only the tracked practices are visible, and most answers name none of them.</p>
{% endif %}

<h3>Tracked practices only</h3>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th class="num">Prompts where a tracked practice was named in any repetition</th><th class="num">Mean overlap there</th><th class="num">...all repetitions identical</th><th class="num">Practice-prompt pairs named in only some repetitions</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{% for e in d.engines %}{% set v = d.variability[e] %}
<tr><td>{{ labels.get(e, e) }}</td>
<td class="num">{{ v.cells }} <span class="sub">(of {{ v.cells + v.empty_cells }})</span></td>
<td class="num">{{ v.mean_jaccard|pct }}</td>
<td class="num">{{ v.identical_cells }} of {{ v.cells }}</td>
<td class="num">{{ v.mixed }} of {{ v.seen }} ({{ ((v.mixed / v.seen) if v.seen else None)|pct }})</td></tr>
{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small muted">Only prompts where at least one repetition named a tracked practice are compared: two repetitions that both name none say nothing about stability. The last column counts practices that appeared at least once for a prompt and engine and asks how often they did not appear every time: a single run would have said "named" or "not named" depending on luck.</p>

{% for e in d.engines %}{% if d.examples.get(e) %}
<h3>Same question, {{ d.examples[e].reps|length }} runs: {{ labels.get(e, e) }}</h3>
<p class="small">“{{ d.examples[e].prompt }}”</p>
<ul class="reps small">
{% for rep in d.examples[e].reps %}<li>Run {{ rep.rep }}: {% if rep.named %}{% for bid in rep.named %}{{ brands[bid].name }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% else %}<span class="muted">no tracked practice named</span>{% endif %}</li>{% endfor %}
</ul>
{% endif %}{% endfor %}

<h2>3. Most cited sources</h2>
<p class="small muted">Share of all scored answers (all engines) whose citations include the domain. Directory and review sites often outrank practices' own websites: that is where the work is.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Type</th><th class="num">Answers citing it</th><th class="num">Share</th>{% for e in d.engines %}<th class="num">{{ e }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>
<tbody>
{% for t in d.top_domains %}
<tr><td>{{ t.domain }}</td><td>{{ t.category }}</td><td class="num">{{ t.runs }}</td><td class="num">{{ t.share|pct }}</td>{% for e in d.engines %}<td class="num">{{ t.by_engine.get(e, 0) }}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>
{% else %}<tr><td colspan="9" class="muted">No citations recorded.</td></tr>
{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>

{% if d.other_hidden %}
<h3>Other businesses named</h3>
<p class="small muted">The engines also named {{ d.other_distinct }} other distinct business names (found by the LLM judge). They are not listed in this anonymized version.</p>
{% elif d.other_businesses %}
<h3>Other businesses named{% if d.other_heuristic %} (heuristic){% else %} (LLM judge){% endif %}</h3>
{% if d.other_heuristic %}<p class="small muted">No LLM judge ran. These are bold or linked phrases that look like business names and are not on the tracked list, with the number of answers that include them. Expect some noise; this shows who else the engines name, since the tracked list is only a convenience sample.</p>{% endif %}
<p class="small">{% for n, c in d.other_businesses %}{{ n }} ({{ c }}){% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}</p>
{% endif %}

<h2>4. Prompt-level detail</h2>
<p class="small muted">Counts of answers naming each practice, per prompt and engine (k/n).</p>
{% for b in cfg.brands %}
<details><summary>{{ b.name }}</summary>
<div class="scroll"><table class="hm">
<thead><tr><th>Prompt</th>{% for e in d.engines %}<th>{{ e }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>
<tbody>
{% for row in d.heat[b.id] %}<tr><td style="text-align:left">{{ row.prompt }}</td>{% for c in row.cells %}<td>{{ c.k }}/{{ c.n }}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div></details>
{% endfor %}

<h2>5. Method and limits</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>None of these is the consumer app.</b> Each engine is an official API, a vendor's coding-agent CLI, or (for Google) a search-data provider, with web search on and the location set to {{ d.meta.location }}. A real patient in the ChatGPT, Claude, Perplexity or Gemini app may see something different: the apps add their own system prompts, use device location and may use history or memory. Treat these numbers as a clean, non-personalised proxy, and compare with answers pasted from the apps (<code>vistrack import</code>).</li>
<li><b>What each engine is.</b><ul>{% for e in d.engines %}<li><b>{{ labels[e] }}</b>: {{ notes[e]|safe }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul></li>
<li><b>Models used.</b> {% for e in d.engines %}{{ labels.get(e, e) }}: <code>{{ d.meta.models[e]|join(', ') }}</code>{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}. Each run stores model, tool version, timestamp, location, the raw JSON response and its SHA-256.</li>
{% if 'google_aio' in d.engines or 'google_ai_mode' in d.engines %}<li><b>Google is third-party.</b> AI Overviews and AI Mode come from DataForSEO. AI Overviews do not always appear; when none is shown the run is recorded and excluded from the denominator.</li>{% endif %}
<li><b>Gemini is not measured.</b> Its grounding terms prohibit storing and analysing results.</li>
<li><b>Errors are not "not mentioned".</b> Failed or rate-limited calls are retried, then stored as errors and left out of every rate. Errors in this batch: {% for e in d.engines %}{{ e }} {{ d.engine_summary[e].errors }}/{{ d.engine_summary[e].attempted }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.</li>
<li><b>Mention = the practice is named</b> (alias match on normalised text), including negative mentions, which are flagged separately. <b>Cited</b> = a cited URL belongs to the practice's domain.</li>
<li><b>Intervals.</b> 95% Wilson per cell. Each rate pools {{ cfg.prompts|length }} prompts × the engine's repetitions ({% for e in d.engines %}{{ labels[e] }}: {{ d.engine_summary[e].scored }} answers{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}); overlapping intervals mean the difference is not established. Per-prompt cells (section 4) have only {{ d.engine_summary.values()|map(attribute='reps')|min }}–{{ d.engine_summary.values()|map(attribute='reps')|max }} runs and very wide intervals. The by-prompt interval is a percentile bootstrap over {{ cfg.prompts|length }} prompts, centred on the mean of per-prompt rates; it undercovers with so few prompts, so use it as a warning, not a bound.</li>
<li><b>No causal claims.</b> This is a snapshot; it says nothing about why a practice is or is not named.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Extractor validation</h3>
{% if validation_real %}
<p><b>On real answers:</b> {{ validation_real.n_items }} answers from this batch, labelled by a model (Claude) that read each one in full, before seeing any extractor output; {{ validation_real.n_items_with_a_tracked_brand }} of them name a tracked practice ({{ validation_real.n_true_mentions }} true mentions in total). The labels are <b>not yet spot-checked by a human</b>. Precision / recall on mentions:</p>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Method</th><th class="num">Precision</th><th class="num">Recall</th><th class="num">Correct</th><th class="num">False positives</th><th class="num">Missed</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{% for k, label in (('regex', 'Alias regex alone'), ('judge', 'LLM judge alone (Claude Haiku)'), ('either', 'Regex or judge'), ('both', 'Regex and judge')) %}{% set m = validation_real[k] %}
<tr><td>{{ label }}</td><td class="num">{{ m.precision|pct }}</td><td class="num">{{ m.recall|pct }}</td><td class="num">{{ m.tp }}</td><td class="num">{{ m.fp }}</td><td class="num">{{ m.fn }}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
{% if cfg.notes %}<p class="small"><b>What happened:</b> {{ cfg.notes }}</p>{% endif %}
<p class="small muted">With only {{ validation_real.n_true_mentions }} true mentions in the sample, these percentages are rough: one error moves them by several points. The judge was run on {{ validation_real.n_judged }} of the {{ validation_real.n_items }} labelled answers.</p>
{% endif %}
{% if d.judge_agree and d.judge_agree.values()|map(attribute='runs_judged')|sum %}
<p class="small">Judge vs regex over all judged answers (mention-level): {% for e in d.engines %}{{ labels[e] }}: {{ d.judge_agree[e].both }} agreed, {{ d.judge_agree[e].regex_only }} regex only, {{ d.judge_agree[e].judge_only }} judge only{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}. The tables above use the regex layer.</p>
{% endif %}
{% if validation %}<p class="small"><b>Synthetic edge-case set</b> (written by the author, not real answers):</p>{% endif %}
{% if validation %}
<p>Hand-labelled set of {{ validation.n_items }} answers (written by the author to cover edge cases; they are <em>not</em> real engine outputs). Mention detection: precision <b>{{ validation.mentioned.precision|pct }}</b>, recall <b>{{ validation.mentioned.recall|pct }}</b> ({{ validation.mentioned.tp }} correct, {{ validation.mentioned.fp }} false positive, {{ validation.mentioned.fn }} missed). Citation matching: precision {{ validation.cited.precision|pct }}, recall {{ validation.cited.recall|pct }}. Answers fully right: {{ validation.item_exact_match.k }}/{{ validation.item_exact_match.n }} (95% CI {{ validation.item_exact_match.wilson95[0]|pct }}–{{ validation.item_exact_match.wilson95[1]|pct }}).</p>
{% if validation.mentioned.precision < 0.95 or validation.mentioned.recall < 0.95 %}
<p class="small"><b>Below the 95% bar</b> proposed for reporting mention rates. The misses are generic phrases that look like a name, indirect references and typos; the optional LLM judge (<code>--judge</code>) is meant for these, and its agreement with the regex layer is recorded per mention.</p>
{% endif %}
{% if validation.errors %}
<details><summary>Known extractor errors ({{ validation.errors|length }})</summary><ul class="small">
{% for er in validation.errors %}<li><code>{{ er.id }}</code> {{ er.field }}: {{ er.note }}{% if er.false_positive %} · false positive: {{ er.false_positive|join(', ') }}{% endif %}{% if er.false_negative %} · missed: {{ er.false_negative|join(', ') }}{% endif %}</li>{% endfor %}
</ul></details>
{% endif %}
<p class="small muted">Before relying on real numbers, label ~100 real answers from the first batch (<code>python -m vistrack gold-sample</code>) and re-measure.</p>
{% else %}
<p class="muted">Validation not run (<code>python -m vistrack validate</code>).</p>
{% endif %}
</main>
</body>
</html>
